from author import *
unit(8,'tokens','Tokenization: text becomes an indexed sequence',
'''A transformer consumes integer token IDs, not words. Subword tokenizers split unfamiliar identifiers into known fragments, so checkout_timeout can require several positions while a common word may require one. Whitespace, punctuation and Unicode normalization change the sequence. Vocabulary size is the number of IDs the model recognizes; sequence length is the number of positions in one input. Neither is the number of documents.\n\nFor failure triage, preserving exception names and paths can matter more than lowercasing everything. Compare raw and normalized logs with the actual model tokenizer before choosing preprocessing. Tokens have no universal meaning across models: ID 42 in one vocabulary is unrelated to ID 42 in another.''',
'''The local transformer lab uses a small, declared vocabulary so you can inspect every ID without downloading weights. Production experiments use AutoTokenizer from the model's published checkpoint, preserving attention masks and tokenizer revision alongside weights. Unknown IDs must map to an explicit unknown token rather than silently disappear.\n\nCount prompt, retrieved context and requested completion together before making a request. Truncate on semantic boundaries and preserve the error cause; chopping the tail of a stack trace can remove the most useful evidence.''',
'''A developer estimates cost from len(text.split()) and undercounts long stack traces. Reproduce with URLs, emoji and minified JSON, compare actual token counts, and inspect which evidence was removed. A tokenizer mismatch can also produce valid-looking IDs that feed the wrong embedding rows.\n\nTest encode/decode round trips subject to documented normalization, empty strings, unknown characters, and context overflow. Do not assert that every word is exactly one token.''',
'''Create a token budget report for ten CI logs: model/tokenizer revision, input count, reserved output count and retained evidence. Reject inputs that exceed the budget before provider work. Record both truncation rate and downstream classification accuracy.\n\nA shorter prompt is successful only if it preserves the decision-critical evidence. Compare the same held-out failures with and without normalization, keeping model and prompt versions fixed.''',
"vocab = {'<unk>': 0, 'timeout': 1, 'database': 2}\nids = [vocab.get(token, 0) for token in 'database timeout'.split()]\nassert ids == [2, 1]",
py('Implement encode(text, vocab): split on whitespace and map unknown tokens to vocab["<unk>"].',"def encode(text, vocab):\n    return [vocab.get(t, vocab['<unk>']) for t in text.split()]","assert encode('db timeout', {'<unk>':0,'timeout':1}) == [0,1]\nassert encode('', {'<unk>':0}) == []","def encode(text, vocab):\n    # Keep unknown positions\n    return []"),
('What must match a trained embedding table?',['The tokenizer vocabulary and ID mapping','The number of CI builds','The display font'],0,'Embedding rows are selected by token ID, so the trained mapping is part of the model contract.'),terms=['Token','Vocabulary','Tokenization'])
unit(8,'attention','Attention: query, key and value with a causal mask',
'''Attention asks which other positions should contribute to the current position. A query represents what a position seeks; keys describe what each position offers; values contain the information to aggregate. Compute query-key dot products, scale by the square root of key dimension, apply the permitted-position mask, then softmax each query row.\n\nThe output is a weighted sum of values, not the selected key or a database lookup. In a failure log, the word timeout may attend to database rather than unrelated login text. Learned projections determine this behavior; a diagram illustrates a mechanism, not a proof that a model understands a cause.''',
'''For one query q=[1,0], keys [[1,0],[0,1]] and values [[4,0],[0,2]], unscaled scores are [1,0]. Softmax gives approximately [0.731,0.269], so the output is [2.924,0.538]. Scaling changes the weights. Subtract the maximum score before exponentiating for numerical stability.\n\nDecoder self-attention masks future positions with negative infinity before softmax. Padding masks prevent empty slots from absorbing weight. Each head has its own projections; concatenated heads pass through an output projection, allowing different relationships without assuming each head has a fixed human label.''',
'''A mask applied after softmax without renormalizing leaves a row sum below one. A transposed key matrix or wrong softmax axis mixes batch examples instead of positions. Test shape contracts, finite weights, nonnegative weights and row sums of one.\n\nA decoder trained with visible future tokens can show excellent training loss and fail during generation. Check that perturbing a future token cannot change earlier outputs in evaluation mode.''',
'''Use the attention calculator to vary scores and observe normalized weights and the value mixture. Implement stable softmax and test extreme scores such as [1000,1001]. The stability test is an SDET boundary check with a mathematical invariant.\n\nAttention weights help inspect the computation but are not sufficient explanations of factual correctness. Validate final claims against source evidence and test causal masking independently of answer quality.''',
"import math\ndef softmax(scores):\n    m=max(scores)\n    e=[math.exp(s-m) for s in scores]\n    return [v/sum(e) for v in e]\nprint(softmax([1,0]))",
py('Implement softmax(scores), stable for very large scores. Return [] for no scores.',"import math\ndef softmax(scores):\n    if not scores: return []\n    e=[math.exp(s-max(scores)) for s in scores]\n    return [v/sum(e) for v in e]","assert softmax([])==[]\nassert abs(sum(softmax([1000,1001]))-1)<1e-9\nassert softmax([1,1])==[0.5,0.5]","def softmax(scores):\n    return []"),
('When should a causal mask be applied?',['Before softmax','After returning the answer','Only during logging'],0,'Disallowed scores must receive zero normalized weight; masking the scores achieves that.'),demo='attention',terms=['Attention','Query','Key','Value','Softmax','Causal mask'])
unit(8,'blocks','Transformer blocks: position, residuals and encoder–decoder roles',
'''Attention alone is permutation equivariant: swapping input positions swaps outputs without expressing order. Positional information supplies order through learned embeddings, sinusoidal encodings or rotary mechanisms. A transformer block combines attention with a position-wise feed-forward network, residual connections and normalization. Residual paths let gradients flow without replacing the entire representation at every layer.\n\nAn encoder can see both directions and produce representations for classification or retrieval. A causal decoder predicts the next token from preceding tokens. An encoder-decoder model reads a source sequence and generates a target with cross-attention to encoded source positions.''',
'''Track shapes before code: token IDs [batch,length], hidden vectors [batch,length,width], per-head Q/K/V [batch,heads,length,head_width]. Project width into head dimensions, form scores [batch,heads,length,length], then return to hidden width. Feed-forward layers expand and contract each position's width.\n\nThe runnable lab uses torch.nn.TransformerEncoderLayer and an explicit padding mask. Inspect train/eval behavior because dropout changes repeated outputs. Persist vocabulary, positional convention and maximum length with the checkpoint.''',
'''Confusing an embedding encoder with a generation decoder produces an integration mismatch: sentence vectors are not next-token distributions. Forgetting positional information can make two differently ordered log messages nearly indistinguishable.\n\nA successful tensor shape does not prove a correct mask. Test padding invariance and causal invariance, then compare held-out quality. Length-dependent failures often indicate truncation or positional limits rather than random provider errors.''',
'''Sketch the failure-classification encoder path and the support-answer decoder path with input/output shapes. Explain why cross-attention is appropriate for source-to-target generation and why sentence embeddings need a pooling strategy.\n\nFor an SDET, architecture knowledge enables focused tests: permutation sensitivity, padding invariance, finite outputs, and reproducible evaluation mode. These contracts complement task-level precision and evidence correctness.''',
"import torch\nfrom torch import nn\nlayer=nn.TransformerEncoderLayer(d_model=16,nhead=4,batch_first=True)\nlayer.eval()\nx=torch.zeros(2,5,16)\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    assert layer(x).shape == (2,5,16)",
rubric('Describe tests for a padded log classifier and a causal generator. Include both shape and information-flow checks.',[{'label':'Padding invariance','terms':['padding','unchanged']},{'label':'Causal isolation','terms':['future','earlier']},{'label':'Evaluation behavior','terms':['eval','dropout']}],'Padding changes should leave real-token outputs unchanged under the mask. Changing future tokens must leave earlier decoder outputs unchanged. Use eval mode to disable dropout before comparing.'),
('What supplies order that plain self-attention lacks?',['Positional information','A larger batch alone','JSON parsing'],0,'Position mechanisms distinguish otherwise permutation-equivariant token processing.'),terms=['Transformer','Encoder','Decoder','Residual connection'])
unit(9,'lifecycle','Pretraining, instruction tuning and grounded generation',
'''Pretraining learns a next-token distribution from many sequences. Instruction tuning trains responses to instructions, while preference or reward-based alignment changes which responses are favored. These stages can improve behavior without making the model an authoritative database. A foundation model can be adapted to many tasks; a task classifier produces a narrower output contract.\n\nAt inference, the decoder repeatedly predicts a distribution for the next token and appends a selected token. A fluent explanation of a CI failure can still invent an owner because the training objective rewards plausible sequences rather than verified access to your repository.''',
'''Separate system behavior, user request and untrusted retrieved evidence in the request. Supply current failure records with stable evidence IDs, require an UNKNOWN classification when insufficient evidence exists, and validate typed output. Keep an independent deterministic source lookup for citations.\n\nThe local provider adapter records model, prompt version, token counts and finish reason. Live mode uses environment credentials; offline fixtures are explicitly labeled and never described as a live LLM result.''',
'''A team interprets low temperature as factual accuracy. Repeatedly generating a fabricated owner demonstrates the mistake: stable errors are still errors. Another team treats retrieval text as instructions, enabling indirect prompt injection.\n\nTest missing evidence, contradictory records, stale documentation and refused requests. Score factual claims separately from JSON validity. A valid schema can carry unsupported facts.''',
'''Build a release dataset with known failures, unknown failures and conflicting evidence. Verify that classifications are supported, citations resolve and uncertainty triggers escalation. Report abstention rate alongside accepted-answer accuracy.\n\nUse prompting for behavior, RAG for changing knowledge and fine-tuning for repeated task patterns only after evaluation establishes a need. Version these decisions so a regression can be attributed to a particular change.''',
"result={'category':'UNKNOWN','evidence_ids':[],'needs_review':True}\nassert result['category']!='UNKNOWN' or result['needs_review']",
py('Implement valid_decision(result): UNKNOWN requires needs_review=True; any other category requires at least one evidence_id.',"def valid_decision(result):\n    return result.get('needs_review') is True if result.get('category')=='UNKNOWN' else bool(result.get('evidence_ids'))","assert valid_decision({'category':'UNKNOWN','needs_review':True})\nassert not valid_decision({'category':'UNKNOWN','needs_review':False})\nassert not valid_decision({'category':'PRODUCT_DEFECT','evidence_ids':[]})","def valid_decision(result):\n    return False"),
('Does instruction tuning guarantee factual answers?',['No: external evidence and evaluation remain necessary','Yes: a fluent answer is verified','Only if temperature is zero'],0,'Training changes response behavior; it does not verify current application facts.'),terms=['Pretraining','Instruction tuning','Hallucination'])
unit(9,'decoding','Temperature, top-p and repeatability',
'''Temperature rescales logits before softmax. Lower positive temperature concentrates probability on high-logit tokens; higher temperature spreads it. Top-p keeps the smallest ranked prefix whose cumulative probability reaches p, then renormalizes before sampling. These operations change selection behavior, not the truth of a claim.\n\nGreedy decoding chooses an argmax, yet provider behavior can still vary with model revisions, tie breaking and execution. Reproducibility means controlling and recording the relevant configuration, then measuring the remaining variation.''',
'''Take probabilities [0.6,0.3,0.1]. At p=0.8 the nucleus includes the first two tokens because 0.6 is insufficient and 0.9 reaches the threshold. Their renormalized probabilities are two-thirds and one-third. Sort by descending probability before accumulating.\n\nFor classification, prefer a typed enum with explicit abstention and measure class stability across repeated calls. For test generation, allow variation but measure requirement coverage and executable correctness. Keep generation parameters in the experiment manifest.''',
'''Selecting every token below probability p confuses a per-token threshold with cumulative mass. Applying temperature to already normalized probabilities without the correct transformation changes the distribution incorrectly.\n\nRepeat the same input enough times to estimate variability and record request configuration. Never mark a test flaky merely because wording changes if the semantic contract remains satisfied.''',
'''The decoding calculator exposes temperature and nucleus membership. Implement the nucleus rule with deterministic ordering, check p in (0,1], and test p=1 as a boundary. Record the preserved probability mass before normalization.\n\nAn AI regression gate should test supported claims, forbidden actions and schema rather than exact prose. Exact comparisons remain appropriate for deterministic application outputs such as canonical enums and sorted IDs.''',
"def nucleus(probs,p):\n    ranked=sorted(enumerate(probs),key=lambda x:-x[1])\n    selected=[];mass=0\n    for i,v in ranked:\n        selected.append(i);mass+=v\n        if mass>=p:break\n    return selected",
py('Implement nucleus(probs,p), returning ranked token indices through cumulative p. Reject p outside (0,1].',"def nucleus(probs,p):\n    if not 0<p<=1: raise ValueError('p')\n    out=[];mass=0\n    for i,v in sorted(enumerate(probs),key=lambda x:-x[1]):\n        out.append(i);mass+=v\n        if mass>=p: break\n    return out","assert nucleus([.6,.3,.1],.8)==[0,1]\nassert nucleus([.1,.8,.1],.7)==[1]\ntry: nucleus([1],0); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def nucleus(probs,p):\n    return []"),
('At top-p=.8 for [.6,.3,.1], which tokens remain?',['The first two','Only the first','All tokens smaller than .8'],0,'Cumulative mass must reach .8; .6+.3=.9 is the smallest sufficient prefix.'),demo='decoding',terms=['Temperature','Top-p','Logit'])
unit(9,'selection','Model selection: quality, context, latency and cost',
'''Choose models against a workload contract rather than a popularity ranking. A failure classifier needs enum accuracy, calibration and low latency; a complex investigation needs multi-step evidence use and safe tools. Context capacity is an input/output budget, not a promise that every token is equally usable. Distracting evidence can reduce quality before the nominal limit is reached.\n\nReasoning-oriented models may spend additional computation or tokens internally. Compare observed end-to-end latency and billed usage using the provider's returned accounting, rather than assuming all models have identical controls or output behavior.''',
'''Create a paired benchmark over fixed cases with model ID, provider, prompt hash, tokenizer revision, retrieval version and generation parameters. Measure accepted accuracy, abstention, p95 latency, rate-limit frequency and cost per accepted task. Account for failed requests and retries.\n\nCost=(input_tokens*input_rate + output_tokens*output_rate)/1_000_000 when rates are quoted per million. Treat cached-token discounts and reasoning usage according to returned provider fields. Store rates as dated configuration rather than hardcoded current facts.''',
'''A cheaper model doubles retries and review labor, raising cost per useful answer. A bigger context includes unauthorized documents, increasing both security risk and price. A cache keyed only by user question returns one tenant's answer to another.\n\nTest cache scope using tenant, principal permissions, prompt/model versions and index generation. Distinguish cold and warm latency, and retest after a provider revision even when the public alias stays unchanged.''',
'''Compare candidates on a held-out set and choose a Pareto frontier: no candidate simultaneously improves quality, cost and latency over another. State constraints first, then reject candidates that violate safety or latency limits.\n\nUse the cost calculator to estimate per-call and monthly spend, then replace estimates with measured usage from the local benchmark. A release decision includes uncertainty and a rollback model, not only an average score.''',
"def cost(input_tokens,output_tokens,input_rate,output_rate):\n    return (input_tokens*input_rate+output_tokens*output_rate)/1_000_000\nassert cost(1000,500,2,8)==.006",
py('Implement cost_usd(input_tokens,output_tokens,input_rate,output_rate) for rates per million. Reject negative quantities.',"def cost_usd(input_tokens,output_tokens,input_rate,output_rate):\n    if min(input_tokens,output_tokens,input_rate,output_rate)<0: raise ValueError('negative')\n    return (input_tokens*input_rate+output_tokens*output_rate)/1000000","assert abs(cost_usd(1000,500,2,8)-.006)<1e-12\ntry: cost_usd(-1,0,1,1); assert False\nexcept ValueError: pass","def cost_usd(input_tokens,output_tokens,input_rate,output_rate):\n    return 0"),
('Which cost measure captures retries and abstentions?',['Cost per accepted task','Price per token alone','Prompt character count'],0,'Useful-task economics include all attempts and the rate of accepted outcomes.'),demo='cost',terms=['Context window','Token budget','Pareto frontier'])
